# Git and PR conventions

## Commits

Use Conventional Commits:

- `feat:` — new user-facing capability
- `fix:` — bug fix
- `chore:` — tooling, deps, busywork
- `docs:` — documentation / ADRs
- `refactor:` — no behavior change intended
- `test:` — tests only
- `ci:` — workflows

## Branching

- **No direct commits to `main`.** Feature branches + PR review even for a two-person team.
- **Squash merge** to keep `main` history linear and readable.

## Every PR must include

1. **What** changed
2. **Why** it changed
3. **How** it was tested (commands, CI, manual checks)
4. **Docs** — which `docs/architecture/` pages changed (or N/A with one-line why)

Contract / OpenAPI changes must update `packages/shared-types` and web consumers in the **same PR**.

## Architecture-relevant PRs

When the change touches boundaries, endpoints, data model, ingest, admin, reader routes, OpenAPI/shared-types, or hosting/env:

- [ ] Atlas page(s) updated (or N/A with one-line why)
- [ ] Mermaid still accurate
- [ ] “Last verified” bumped on edited pages
- [ ] Hub index updated if a new page was added
